4. Recommended image creation workflow

This is probably the most important part of your KB.

I recommend documenting a workflow like this:

                    ┌───────────────┐
                    │   Base AMI    │
                    │ Windows/Linux │
                    └───────┬───────┘
                            ↓
                    ┌───────────────┐
                    │   Customize   │
                    │   Components  │
                    └───────┬───────┘
                            ↓
                    ┌───────────────┐
                    │   Hardening   │
                    │   + Security  │
                    └───────┬───────┘
                            ↓
                    ┌───────────────┐
                    │     Patch     │
                    │     OS        │
                    └───────┬───────┘
                            ↓
                    ┌───────────────┐
                    │    Validate   │
                    │   Build Tests │
                    └───────┬───────┘
                            ↓
                    ┌───────────────┐
                    │  Create AMI   │
                    │  Version X.Y.Z│
                    └───────┬───────┘
                            ↓
                    ┌───────────────┐
                    │  Test Golden  │
                    │      AMI      │
                    └───────┬───────┘
                            ↓
                    ┌───────────────┐
                    │    Approval   │
                    └───────┬───────┘
                            ↓
                    ┌───────────────┐
                    │  Distribution │
                    │ Regions/Acct  │
                    └───────────────┘

AWS Image Builder naturally maps to this model through Build → Test → Distribution workflows.

Step 1 — Select base AMI

Example:

Windows Server 2022
        OR
RHEL 9
        OR
Ubuntu 24.04

You need to decide:

Who provides the base image?
Is it an AWS-managed image?
Is it a Marketplace image?
Is it internally maintained?
How often is the base image updated?

The base image becomes the foundation of your Golden AMI.

Step 2 — Add Image Builder components

This is where you customize the image.

AWS Image Builder uses components to perform build and test actions. Components are based on YAML documents executed by AWS Task Orchestrator and Executor (AWSTOE).

For example:

Component 1
Install Windows updates

Component 2
Install New Relic

Component 3
Install security agent

Component 4
Configure Windows security settings

Component 5
Configure logging

Component 6
Install required tools

You should investigate which components your organization actually needs.

Documentation

AWS — Use components to customize Image Builder images

Step 3 — Harden the OS

This is where your security baseline comes in.

Your KB should explain that the Golden AMI must follow the organization's approved security standard.

Potential areas:

OS Hardening
   ├── Password policies
   ├── Firewall
   ├── Services
   ├── Audit policies
   ├── Logging
   ├── Encryption
   ├── Security agents
   └── Vulnerability remediation

Important: Don't invent the actual corporate security settings.

If your company has:

CIS benchmark
Security baseline
Vulnerability management standard
Windows hardening standard
Linux hardening standard

reference those documents from your KB.

Step 4 — Patch

The Golden AMI should have an explicitly documented patching process.

For example:

Base AMI
   ↓
Check latest approved patches
   ↓
Install patches
   ↓
Reboot if required
   ↓
Verify patch level
   ↓
Continue build

You need to establish whether the company wants:

Monthly Golden AMI builds
Build on Patch Tuesday
Build whenever critical vulnerabilities appear
Scheduled automatic builds

This becomes part of the maintenance standard.

Step 5 — Test

Don't just create an AMI and assume it works.

AWS Image Builder has a dedicated Test stage where it launches an instance from the newly created image and runs test components.

You should define a test checklist.

Basic tests
✓ EC2 launches
✓ Instance passes status checks
✓ OS boots
✓ Login works
✓ Disk configuration correct
✓ Network works
Software tests
✓ Monitoring agent running
✓ Security agent running
✓ SSM available
✓ Required services running
Security tests
✓ Required patches installed
✓ Security baseline applied
✓ No critical vulnerability
✓ Firewall configured
Application tests

If the AMI contains application-specific software:

✓ Application starts
✓ Required ports available
✓ Dependencies available

AWS allows multiple test workflows and test groups, including running tests in parallel.

